WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:04.880
Starting with my first job in high-school,
I spent years working for tips.

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:09.940
Serving coffee, waiting tables, and parking
cars, I knew the majority of my daily pay

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:13.140
would technically be an “optional” gratuity.

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:15.280
It’s kind of weird when you stop to think
about it.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:18.020
Paying your phone bill or mechanic isn’t
optional.

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:22.750
So why is it okay to choose how much your
waitress or taxi driver makes that day?

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:23.920
It’s true.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:28.210
My night could be made - or ruined - by the
actions of a single customer.

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:30.990
Tipping customs are complicated and confusing.

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:36.390
What’s expected and acceptable varies by
country, region, and industry type.

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:41.920
Sometimes a tip is truly something “a little extra”,
and other times it’s how a worker pays their bills.

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:45.700
Here in the U.S. tipping a large amount is
prevalent and expected.

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:47.870
But is that the case around the globe?

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:53.320
And how can you know when - and how much - you
should tip when it’s not completely obvious?

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:05.940
The word “tip” suggests a tiny bit - something
extra.

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:09.610
And in most countries outside of North America,
that’s exactly what it is.

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:13.930
The most common tipping etiquette across the
globe is to leave a little extra change if

00:01:13.930 --> 00:01:16.500
you want to, though it’s certainly not expected.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:20.250
In China a tip will often trigger a reaction
of pleasant surprise.

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:24.100
In European countries like Italy, Greece,
and France you’re expected to round your

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:26.630
bill up (a form of “keep the change”).

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:30.310
Receiving your bill in India, you’ll likely
see that gratuity is included, though you

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:32.900
might tip up to 10% if not.

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:36.590
And in Japan if you want to let your sushi
chef know you loved your meal

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:41.320
don't do it with a tip — you run the risk of seriously
insulting them!

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:46.220
Here in the U.S., along with Mexico and Canada,
a substantially larger “tip” is the primary

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:48.770
form of compensation for hundreds of professions.

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:54.409
By far, American tipping culture is the most
expensive and pervasive out of any country

00:01:54.409 --> 00:02:00.049
in the world, with over 3 million American
workers in professions that rely on tips.

00:02:00.049 --> 00:02:06.220
When I was a waiter, my hourly rate was a
measly $2.13/hr before tips… a Federal minimum

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:08.450
that hasn’t changed in decades!

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:09.450
How did this happen?

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:15.159
Well, it can all be traced back to our country’s
complicated history with class and race.

00:02:15.159 --> 00:02:19.209
The original custom of tipping dates back
to medieval times and is a remnant of the

00:02:19.209 --> 00:02:22.389
relationship between wealthy lords and their
indentured servants.

00:02:22.389 --> 00:02:27.189
At the beginning, a tip was a tiny bit of
extra money given to a serf for a job done

00:02:27.189 --> 00:02:28.549
especially well.

00:02:28.549 --> 00:02:32.639
By the mid-nineteenth century, the practice
had largely died out, as many workers and

00:02:32.639 --> 00:02:37.370
businesses refused tips because they were
viewed as condescending or a form of bribery.

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:41.519
But then something happened that brought
tipping back in a big way.

00:02:41.519 --> 00:02:46.430
To explain it, let’s hear from Evelyn from
the PBS digital series “Say It Loud”.

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:47.430
Yup!

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:48.829
We’re talking about slavery.

00:02:48.829 --> 00:02:54.489
While the 13th amendment legally abolished
it, that didn’t mean millions of newly freed

00:02:54.489 --> 00:02:58.870
people instantly ascended into that deluxe
apartment in the sky.

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:02.040
Their options for employment were very limited.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:07.129
Those who didn’t work on farms as sharecroppers
entered into the service industry, becoming

00:03:07.129 --> 00:03:12.269
railroad porters, household servants, or…waiters
in restaurants.

00:03:12.269 --> 00:03:17.030
While most states frowned upon tipping—even
making it illegal in some cases, when it came

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:18.460
to restaurants?

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:19.900
Especially in the South?!

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:22.000
Tipping was a loophole.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:25.280
Free labor was the economic backbone of the region.

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:26.820
Why give it up now?!

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:32.339
By the late 1920s, those anti-tipping laws
were overturned, and restaurants stopped paying

00:03:32.339 --> 00:03:37.469
Black employees a wage, forcing them to supplement
their income with tips.

00:03:37.469 --> 00:03:42.900
It was no longer a nice bonus for excellent
service - it was the only way people got paid.

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:47.370
And even though the fight for civil rights
made steps to improve the lives of Black people

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:53.519
post Civil War, this particular practice stuck,
extending to restaurant workers of all shades

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:59.940
- even in the North, turning our uncomfortable
legacy of forced servitude into an industry standard.

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:05.160
To this day, the custom of tipping still contributes
to all sorts of social problems.

00:04:05.169 --> 00:04:09.819
Pieces by The Guardian, The New York Times,
The Economist, and even Adam Ruins Everything

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:16.420
show how tipping in the U.S. contributes to
racial profiling, sexual harassment, and worker poverty.

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:20.940
Strong arguments are made that businesses
would profit more and customers would be happier

00:04:20.949 --> 00:04:23.419
if tipping went the way of the horse &amp; buggy.

00:04:23.419 --> 00:04:27.759
But don’t pull a “Mr. Pink” and make
a social statement by stiffing your waitress.

00:04:27.759 --> 00:04:31.949
This puts the blame and responsibility squarely
on the victim and won’t do anything but

00:04:31.949 --> 00:04:33.940
hurt your cause and make you look cheap.

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:39.189
Love it or hate it, tips-as-wages are still
a widely accepted norm in America.

00:04:39.189 --> 00:04:40.939
You might think you’re a generous tipper.

00:04:40.939 --> 00:04:44.990
Or maybe you think tipping should always be
optional and it’s no biggie to stiff the

00:04:44.990 --> 00:04:47.020
staff if you were unhappy with something.

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:50.750
But are there actually standard rules when
it comes to tipping?

00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:55.300
Turns out there are, and the Emily Post Institute
offers an insight into one of the most confusing

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:57.170
etiquette norms in our society:

00:04:57.170 --> 00:04:59.070
Let’s start with some basics.

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:04.690
Average to good service at restaurants, bars,
hairdressers, cab drivers and spas comes with

00:05:04.690 --> 00:05:09.449
an expectation of a 15-25% standard gratuity.

00:05:09.449 --> 00:05:15.490
A tip of 10% in America is generally reflective
of BAD service, and a tip of nothing really

00:05:15.490 --> 00:05:17.349
isn’t acceptable for any reason.

00:05:17.349 --> 00:05:21.270
If you’re that dissatisfied with the service,
you should let the manager know the reason

00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:25.020
ahead of time and your plan to leave a reduced
tip.

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:26.639
From here it can get complicated.

00:05:26.639 --> 00:05:29.560
What if you dine at a restaurant without a
server?

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:32.620
Or the tip-line that’s started appearing
at neighborhood markets?

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:36.189
What’s expected if you have a shuttle driver
carry your luggage?

00:05:36.189 --> 00:05:40.030
Here are some important exceptions to standard
tipping rules in the U.S.

00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:43.330
Tipping 10% is acceptable at a buffet restaurant.

00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:45.759
Getting food delivered to your home or work?

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:47.740
10-15% is standard.

00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:52.259
If there’s a tip-jar or other counter-service
option to tip, there is no obligation, though

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:56.360
it’s advised to tip “occasionally” if
you’re a regular or your barista or clerk

00:05:56.360 --> 00:05:58.780
provides something a little extra.

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:04.580
A tip of $2-5 is expected for Bellhops, valet
drivers, a doorman or shuttle driver who helps

00:06:04.580 --> 00:06:05.880
you with your luggage.

00:06:05.889 --> 00:06:10.240
Plan for 10-20% for a ride-share service like
Uber or Lyft.

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:15.280
There are even special circumstances when
a tip may be more optional than it seems.

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:19.320
Restaurant employees who handle take-out orders
and bartenders at weddings tend to have a

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:23.630
higher hourly wage or rely on tips much less
or not at all.

00:06:23.630 --> 00:06:28.900
Point-of-sales apps more and more frequently
have a line for tipping, and even a suggested

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:31.340
amount of 15-25%.

00:06:31.340 --> 00:06:36.600
This has led to a phenomenon of “tip-creep”,
where people have begun tipping for non-traditional

00:06:36.610 --> 00:06:39.599
services out of fear or guilt of being cheap.

00:06:39.599 --> 00:06:44.710
Sales Apps like Square have defaulted to prompting
us for tips everywhere from ice-cream parlors

00:06:44.710 --> 00:06:47.949
to farmers’ markets… even on girl scout
cookies!

00:06:47.949 --> 00:06:52.270
If you’re confused or worried about a tip-prompt
on one of these apps, you’re not alone.

00:06:52.270 --> 00:06:56.939
I tend to give 10% if I’m not sure what
the etiquette is, but then I’ll research

00:06:56.939 --> 00:07:00.999
it afterwards by checking the company’s
website, or asking friends I know who work

00:07:00.999 --> 00:07:02.290
in the industry.

00:07:02.290 --> 00:07:06.449
And I know it might seem awkward, but you
can always just ask the person helping you

00:07:06.449 --> 00:07:07.490
then and there.

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:10.990
When I worked for tips, I would’ve preferred
that to getting stiffed.

00:07:10.990 --> 00:07:15.310
Most importantly, if an appropriate tip might
bust your budget, it’s worth pausing to

00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:16.930
re-think your needs.

00:07:16.930 --> 00:07:22.449
Enjoying luxury services like dinners out,
salons, and high-end massages, it’s on you

00:07:22.449 --> 00:07:24.949
to factor in a gratuity for those who served
you.

00:07:24.949 --> 00:07:30.360
If you can afford these discretionary purchases,
it’s worth integrating regular tipping into

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:32.800
your relationship with service professionals.

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:36.600
And now that you’ve watched this video,
you can’t claim you didn’t know any better!

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:38.220
And that’s our two cents.

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:42.920
Thanks to Evelyn for contributing to this
episode, check out her show Say it Loud on

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:45.580
Youtube, the link is in the description.

